A||||6||racefan||Why Iron Duke?||||5||
Z||000000||racefan||11-03-2001||12:28 AM||||Does anyone know how they got the name "Iron Duke" for the 2.5L 4 cylinder? Just wondering ...||||reg||5
Z||000001||Pheagey||11-03-2001||01:52 AM||||As I beleive the heads were made from Iron (how I dunno-drop forged, power fordged, casted...). As for where duke came from I dunno either (my guess: john Wayne a.k.a 'the duke'=tough).<BR>My 2c<BR><P>------------------<BR>"There is no replacement for displacement.Period"<BR>    -Mr.Frosty<P>'88 coupe, 5 speed<P>Http://www.LithiumEffects.com/car.html<P>Killed: '97 Civic LX Auto,<BR>Killed by: '93 Civic EX 5 speed,||||reg||2
Z||000002||MrPBody||11-03-2001||09:38 AM||||I think Napoleon's opponent, the Duke of Wellington, was the original Iron Duke.<P>The "Iron Duke" 4-cylinder is made of cast iron, which is no distinction among engines.  I have no idea why it bears the name.  It sounds like marketing BS.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Rodrv6||11-03-2001||10:42 AM||||It is a marketing thing.  The name was given to the engine in the late 70's or early 80's when GM started using it in the Chevy Monza. Since this car was a replacement for the Vega, and the Vega's aluminum engine was best known for its ability to burn large amounts of oil and wearing out rather quickly, GM wanted to emphasize that the "new" engine in the Monza was good ol' cast iron and would last much longer.  Hence the name "Iron Duke".  The name has stuck very well  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Rod Schneider, Woodstock, Ga.  <BR>White 88 GT :)||||reg||1
Z||000004||Tryxalon||11-03-2001||09:38 PM||||Was "Iron Duke" a marketing thing? <P>Thought it was known for endurance?<BR>Funny, maybe memory going bad, but I think there was something more to it - some award or something in the early 70's on the FIRST version of the engine.  <BR>  <p>[This message has been edited by Tryxalon (edited 11-03-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||cid_colada||11-03-2001||11:12 PM||||I read someplace that the name "Iron Duke" not only had to do with that the engine was made of iron, but the term Duke had something to do with the company that cast the conecting rods... I may be wrong but that is what I read.<P>Black 84 SE||||reg||1
Z||000006||delSol97||11-04-2001||03:35 AM||||They should have called it the "Iron Duck". It would be more appropriate.<BR>||||reg||4
